  "topic_id": "gold-allergy-anaphylaxis",
  "title": "Allergy / Anaphylaxis",
  "markdown": "# Allergy / Anaphylaxis\n\n**IV** EPINEPHrine infusion drip, administered via a Maine EMS-approved medication pump, should be started for patients unresponsive to **IM** EPINEPHrine administration in either of the following settings:\n\n* Cardiovascular collapse (hypotension with altered mental status, pallor, diaphoresis, or delayed capillary refill)\n\n**OR**\n\n* Hypotension that is unresponsive to a total of 60 mL/kg (3 x 20 mL/kg boluses) isotonic fluid boluses and repeat doses of **IM** EPINEPHrine\n\nEvidence does not support the prophylactic use of EPINEPHrine in the asymptomatic patient after exposure to a known allergen.\n\nChildren experiencing cutaneous signs of an allergic reaction with no systemic symptoms should be evaluated by a medical clinician.\n\nChildren experiencing anaphylaxis in the prehospital setting who receive EPINEPHrine require EMS transport.\n",
  "plain_text": "Allergy / Anaphylaxis IV EPINEPHrine infusion drip, administered via a Maine EMS-approved medication pump, should be started for patients unresponsive to IM EPINEPHrine administration in either of the following settings: Cardiovascular collapse (hypotension with altered mental status, pallor, diaphoresis, or delayed capillary refill) OR Hypotension that is unresponsive to a total of 60 mL/kg (3 x 20 mL/kg boluses) isotonic fluid boluses and repeat doses of IM EPINEPHrine Evidence does not support the prophylactic use of EPINEPHrine in the asymptomatic patient after exposure to a known allergen. Children experiencing cutaneous signs of an allergic reaction with no systemic symptoms should be evaluated by a medical clinician. Children experiencing anaphylaxis in the prehospital setting who receive EPINEPHrine require EMS transport.",
